rm in a Long Island district that leans Republican by about four points. He enters the general election with a decisive financial edge, roughly $3.3 million on hand against about $172,000 for Democrat Chris Gallant, and the backing of a rating consensus that ranges from Lean R to Solid R.\n\nGallant's campaign was disrupted in mid-September when reports surfaced of past bondage-gear modeling, which he confirmed and called a political hit; the story dominated coverage of the race. Prediction markets price his chances near 17 percent. With no reliable general-election polling, the race rests on fundamentals, which point to a likely but not certain Republican hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1953, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.158, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1553, "margin": -7.0}]}